 thought it'd be  very serious but he's saying still rock  and roll to me by Billy Joel anyone the  most inspirational trophy and while he's  walking out the door he handed me the  script and I was sold look Keith you  were in get out and you think how can I  match that what can I look for how did  they approach you with this script and  say this is your next big thing  um boots put it very plainly to me you  didn't get out do it again no ok yeah  get out wasn't even now it was it was  just uh I was at Sundance dancing around  in the Sun and there was a strange man  with an afro and mutton chops who was  following me around with oh it was boots  sorry and once I took the pages from him  and read it I realized after a while  that this was something that was very  special and could be an opportunity to  explore a character that hadn't explored  before and tell a story that I hadn't  seen been to been told before and so I  was very excited at the opportunity that  we might be able to make something out  of this crazy thing I'd been looking for  an opportunity to sort of mix it up and  challenge myself and hopefully audiences  and so I thought this was a great  opportunity also to have conversations  about difficult topics and difficult  things to talk about sometimes it's  easier to come up with a disposition  about a thing and when you're seeing it  through art Terry Crews you're the king  of all media you can do anything you  want so I got a shot of that  [Laughter]  you you keep at it I'll come right back  to your pouting so so how how were you  approached for this role and you take a  lot of persuading first of all I have to  give it up to Nina  Bon Jovi because we met uh I was I was  introduced to her and she was so excited  I about this project I got this movie  you won't believe boots Riley and I new  boots and and I also had met the Keith  oh I like a year and a half two years  earlier and I said man I want to work  with you one thing about always been a  big fan but her energy her love she was  like you gotta see this you got it and I  said okay okay I looked at it and I was  like what in the world what and I kept  flipping pages like oh what what and  when I got to the end I would have paid  boots to be in the movie because I said  there's no way I said when do I show up  I will do a free whatever I'll hand out  stuff I'll do security we got to make  sure this gets done and then all then  all the other names started coming in I  didn't even know about all the other  giant names that were in here but again  with her energy and her love for it I  mean that's what a producer is all about  and then like Heath being that catalyst  who I knew would be the future of what  this town is all about this man has got  so much coming it's incredible  Patton Oswalt when did you come on board  as the voice of the mysterious man in  the bowler hat in the wyatt earp  mustache I feel like I came on board  years ago kay let me read the screenplay  years ago and I could in the screenplay  it's the same quality when I read it as  listening to the COO which is the COO my  writing partner I always said it's the  worst music - right - because your  constant wait would rewind that what the  hell like you you're you're it takes you  out of whatever you're working on it's  so dense and amazing and the script when  I the first time you showed it to me I  kept stopping on what in the I would go  back which I've always heard as a script  should not have that quality but I was  and  actually one thing makes me wonder  because I was river reading going I just  want to see this movie just to see if he  can pull these visuals off I remember  reading going how is he going to shoot  what he's describing so that that is  what so I just said I remember telling  you I don't care what you want me to do  in this movie if you ever get this made  I'm in I'll do whatever you tell me then  and Patton came on when there was no  producers yet no money no nothing and I  definitely used his name to the fullest  this film has elements of Fable its  political parody in some ways was it the  film you started out with how much did  it change when I started out I was like  I know I'm gonna make a film that will  be in the telemarketing world and I know  that he's gonna have to there's gonna be  a struggle that he has to decide what  side he's on I knew that I knew the  first scene because that's my friend Rob  ebo that's how he always got his jobs  and he never got caught and then and and  I knew the compliment argument scene  because that happened to my little  brother some years ago and I was always  like I'm gonna put that in a movie one  day and so I that's all I knew but and  then the rest I took the journey with  Cash's I mean I had things that I wanted  to do I wanted there to be a I wanted  there to be a character a black  character that wasn't only thinking  about survival and you know how to make  it through the jungle of other black  people like so many movies are I wanted  it to be what humans do someone that  thinks about himself in the context of  time and space and is thinking about  their existence because I think that's  what all humans doing for me having that  in there I knew that would make him more  human and understandable and but as I  went on see so for me a lot of my work  even with music has been fighting cliche  and I realized that we lean towards  shei all the time because we've seen so  many movies and heard so many songs that  it's hard to parse out what our real  experience is in life from what we saw  in a movie so for instance I've never  been a deli in India I have a clear  picture in my head of deli with the  transportation and the the clothing and  what the streets look like I haven't  even seen a documentary about Delhi all  that stuff is from James Bond movies or  something like that and so if I were to  sit down and write a scene happening in  Delhi it would have not only cliches but  probably very racist things that you  know have to do with that and so when  I'm writing a scene it's all about me  trying it or or anything it's me trying  to figure out parse out what I believe  based on my own experience is me  interrogating myself and based on the  things that I believe that aren't from  you know another movie so I'm you know  for me a lot of that has to do with my  larger ideas about the world and so how  do you have a philosophical idea  injected into a scene do you have  somebody say well you know message  message message no hopefully not and  then do you you know you can come up  with all sorts of contrivances but what  I found and this is just this is how the  weird [ __ ] started getting in there was  like I found that the way that I that  that the way that worked to put those  ideas in in order to keep it more real  in my viewpoint from my experience was  to bend the reality of the world in the  movie and so I didn't set out to make  something weird and stuff like that but  it was it was needed I needed the equi  sapiens there for him to see what he  what it was because I had written the  the rap performance and I thought that  was gonna be the thing for him to see  himself but I was like that doesn't make  sense that that's enough there's slavery  going on and he's selling slaves  and everybody accepts all that so all of  these things were stuff that was new it  had to feel real to me so that that's  the part that changed the other you know  there were other things that changed  especially in the Sundance labs but  mainly around him around me making him  take ownership of his choices  the code-switching elements of this are  familiar to you but they may be really  new to an audience the idea that oh my  god you have to speak in a different  voice to get in different groups to  listen to you so as as you developed  your own characters what did you draw on  I don't think it's that unfamiliar if  you think about it  code switching is something that we all  do to survive in many forms and fashions  and I think the white voice here could  be representative of a lot of different  things not simply just a voice in a way  that sounds over the phone but how we  make ourselves look to others how we  dress ourselves up and pretty little  things and things we do to make  ourselves more palatable particularly in  the workplace and I think everyone that  is involved in that game to one level or  another because it's important to be and  it's has been important for our survival  for years to have that so if you look at  it more as a metaphor everyone is  experiencing that to some degree so I  think it is it's a universal theme and  so I just I really tried to pay less  attention to the implications behind and  more attention to just the idea and then  the ambition that the the character was  experiencing and trying to bring about  change in his life  by any means necessary thereby sometimes  through that overlooking sometimes  friends and family and loved ones in the  process and for Terry know I and I agree  with the Keith and that um it's  definitely the code switching is  something that we all have done in some  way form or fashion but IIIi I think to  define it a little bit better sometimes  code switching sounds like it's some  sort of farce or some kind of game I  think for me it's always survival right  well you know just trying to trick  someone into  selling something or whatever that's my  always saw code-switching being I always  looked at as being bilingual just simply  because I remember being a kid and going  over my white friends houses and I knew  how they lived already simply because  everything was white the media the TV  shows i watch The Brady Bunch  I knew it was like and but then when  they come over my house they didn't  understand what we were saying they had  no clue and they were telling me what  did that mean  what that you get 50 million questions  from your white friends when they come  in your house and you realize oh they  don't know what I'm talking about so I  have to speak their language all the  time if I'm outside I have to speak your  language but there was a lot of times  there was a total unwillingness for them  to learn mine and so you when you talk  about code switching it's about survival  in a lot of ways thank you everybody  please thank the panel

